Import "Epictetus.lingua"

Epicurus: desire, pleasure, and the fear of death (@greek-epicurus: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-schools-life, #source @greek-source-diogenes, #philosophy @greek-epictetus, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-epictetus) { r_6KHZKEFRKY1J18Z7TSRAJABZQP
**Read:** [Diogenes Laertius, Lives and Opinions](../sources/Lives-and-Opinions-Yonge.lingua#r_2MZ8K8NBGJSS2844MJ7DDGR849), Book X: the letter to Menoeceus, printed Menœceus in this translation, and the concluding maxims. Begin with the letter, then compare the maxims on pleasure, desire, prudence, and friendship. For an extended account of nature, return to the letter to Herodotus in the same book.

**Preparation:** [happiness, soul, and the meanings assigned to a term](../foundations/Terms-and-translation.lingua#r_1VGPFMXZ4HHAREA3BAHTSSQQ8D). [Gorgias](../socratic/Gorgias.lingua#r_5G884J0SFXCP4YRBFF5C5YSYD2) and [Phaedo](../plato/Phaedo.lingua#r_6JFZZP5W43FY7FFQENQNH96AFQ) supply useful comparisons. [The atomists](../presocratics/Atomists.lingua#r_666ZSW8DNDQ45TJKB8K9HKVS5B) supports the account of nature if you choose that depth.

Distinguish claiming that pleasure is central to a good life from recommending the pursuit of every immediately attractive experience. Examine how the classification of desires and the consideration of consequences qualify the recommendation.

Reconstruct the argument about death and sensation. Ask what account of harm it assumes and whether a critic could claim a loss without a later experience of that loss. Compare its premises with a chosen argument in Phaedo rather than simply recording opposite conclusions.

**Practice:** choose an ordinary desire. Explain whether satisfying, postponing, or revising it would be recommended under the account, and why. Then propose a case involving friendship, future opportunity, or discomfort that challenges a simplistic reading.

**Check:** defend an interpretation through the letter and a relevant maxim. Identify one dependence between the ethical recommendation and the account of nature or knowledge. Diogenes is the later transmitting author; distinguish the preserved letter from the biographical anecdotes surrounding it.

**Compare next:** [Epictetus](Epictetus.lingua#r_35EWPVCFYNWZ0DYG5R5HM05TC2) or [an investigation of death or desire](../questions/Comparative-investigation.lingua#r_20BK69Q76HJXAGZ5J1T097X29G). **Return:** [Schools of life](Schools-of-life.lingua#r_58ENCWSNQARKCDXEP8FVWY9507) · [Ancient Greek philosophy](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3).
